test modular_polynomial::tests::modular_polynomial_storage_scaling_barrier ... 
=== Modular polynomial Φ_l storage requirements (log_2 scale) ===

       log_2(l)     log_2(num_coefs)    log_2(total_bits)
              1                    2                    5
              2                    4                   10
              3                    6                   15
              4                    8                   19
              8                   16                   36
             16                   32                   69
             32                   64                  134
             64                  128                  263
            128                  256                  520
            256                  512                 1033

For P-256, l = p ≈ 2²⁵⁶ ⇒ log_2(l) = 256.
Storage in bits ≈ 2^1033

That's more bits than there are particles in 10⁵⁰⁰ universes.

Conclusion: Φ_p is COMPUTATIONALLY INACCESSIBLE at P-256 scale.
Any canonical-lift attack on P-256 must avoid materialising Φ_p.
Known approaches: Satoh-Skjernaa-Taguchi uses Φ_l for small l
via a modular tower — but only works for F_{p^n}, not F_p
with prime p.

**This module ships Φ_2 and Φ_3 as fully-instantiated polynomials.**
Higher Φ_l (Φ_5, Φ_7, Φ_11, Φ_13, Φ_17) computable via q-series
but require ~hours of compute and large coefficient tables.
